{
  "version": 1,
  "courseId": 1362070,
  "sourceLanguage": "en",
  "targetLanguage": "zh-TW",
  "lectures": [
    {
      "id": "38345176",
      "title": "Revisiting Variables & Values",
      "sourceHash": "29647321c78473d111ed0daafbbea06339cdc13bd4990f461b1deec135d5273f",
      "cues": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "start": 2.1,
          "end": 4.89,
          "en": "So with the import and export keywords",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "start": 4.89,
          "end": 9.06,
          "en": "and the related concepts, we already explored a bunch",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "start": 9.06,
          "end": 12.57,
          "en": "of crucial JavaScript concepts and features",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "start": 12.57,
          "end": 15.27,
          "en": "which we'll use heavily throughout this course.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "start": 15.27,
          "end": 17.07,
          "en": "But of course, for this refresher",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 6,
          "start": 17.07,
          "end": 19.23,
          "en": "I now also want to take a step back",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 7,
          "start": 19.23,
          "end": 23.37,
          "en": "and also explore some other foundational JavaScript features",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 8,
          "start": 23.37,
          "end": 25.14,
          "en": "and concepts like working",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 9,
          "start": 25.14,
          "end": 29.55,
          "en": "with variables, values, functions, objects, and much more.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 10,
          "start": 29.55,
          "end": 32.25,
          "en": "And it definitely makes sense to start",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 11,
          "start": 32.25,
          "end": 36.21,
          "en": "with variables, values, and operators, for that.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 12,
          "start": 36.21,
          "end": 38.01,
          "en": "In this app js file, I'll comment",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 13,
          "start": 38.01,
          "end": 40.243,
          "en": "out all this code we wrote before",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 14,
          "start": 40.243,
          "end": 43.29,
          "en": "because we don't need it here, and instead",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 15,
          "start": 43.29,
          "end": 47.28,
          "en": "I now do want to revisit some foundational concepts related",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 16,
          "start": 47.28,
          "end": 50.73,
          "en": "to variables and values and operators.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 17,
          "start": 50.73,
          "end": 53.43,
          "en": "Because in the end, when you're building an app",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 18,
          "start": 53.43,
          "end": 57.54,
          "en": "with JavaScript or with React, which uses JavaScript",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 19,
          "start": 57.54,
          "end": 61.74,
          "en": "of course it's in the end all about data and therefore",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 20,
          "start": 61.74,
          "end": 65.19,
          "en": "in the end values you are working with in your code.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 21,
          "start": 65.19,
          "end": 66.45,
          "en": "If you take a look",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 22,
          "start": 66.45,
          "end": 70.98,
          "en": "at applications like Twitter or Google Maps, those apps",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 23,
          "start": 70.98,
          "end": 74.31,
          "en": "like all apps of course in the end are about data.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 24,
          "start": 74.31,
          "end": 76.83,
          "en": "The tweets you write and read are data.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 25,
          "start": 76.83,
          "end": 78.72,
          "en": "Your location is data.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 26,
          "start": 78.72,
          "end": 81.51,
          "en": "The location you want to go to is data.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 27,
          "start": 81.51,
          "end": 85.14,
          "en": "All these things are data that must be handled",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 28,
          "start": 85.14,
          "end": 88.2,
          "en": "in your code and in your Java script code.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 29,
          "start": 88.2,
          "end": 92.25,
          "en": "You can therefore handle a broad variety of values.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 30,
          "start": 92.25,
          "end": 96.27000000000001,
          "en": "Most importantly, strings, numbers, booleans.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 31,
          "start": 96.27000000000001,
          "end": 99.6,
          "en": "This the special null and undefined values",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 32,
          "start": 99.6,
          "end": 103.08,
          "en": "which simply mean that a certain data container",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 33,
          "start": 103.08,
          "end": 106.59,
          "en": "a certain variable, doesn't contain any values yet.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 34,
          "start": 106.59,
          "end": 109.92,
          "en": "And also the special object value to which I'll get back",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 35,
          "start": 109.92,
          "end": 114.63,
          "en": "later, now, a value in JavaScript can be created",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 36,
          "start": 114.63,
          "end": 116.64,
          "en": "in the place where you need it.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 37,
          "start": 116.64,
          "end": 119.31,
          "en": "For example, if I want to output hello world here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 38,
          "start": 119.31,
          "end": 121.29,
          "en": "I can simply create the string.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 39,
          "start": 121.29,
          "end": 126.29,
          "en": "Hello world with double or single quotes, doesn't matter",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 40,
          "start": 126.54,
          "end": 128.79,
          "en": "but quotes are of course needed.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 41,
          "start": 128.79,
          "end": 132.45,
          "en": "And if I then save this and reload my app",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 42,
          "start": 132.45,
          "end": 135.72,
          "en": "I would see hello world in the console.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 43,
          "start": 135.72,
          "end": 138.57,
          "en": "So I can create values when I need them.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 44,
          "start": 138.57,
          "end": 140.64,
          "en": "For example, here, when I need a value",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 45,
          "start": 140.64,
          "end": 142.68,
          "en": "as an input for this console",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 46,
          "start": 142.68,
          "end": 146.313,
          "en": "log command that's built into the browser in the end.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 47,
          "start": 147.42000000000002,
          "end": 150.32999999999998,
          "en": "But it's also quite common that values should be stored.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 48,
          "start": 150.32999999999998,
          "end": 153.063,
          "en": "And for that, you're using variables.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 49,
          "start": 153.96,
          "end": 158.16,
          "en": "Variables are simply data containers where you store a value",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 50,
          "start": 158.16,
          "end": 161.7,
          "en": "in a variable which carries any name of your choice.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 51,
          "start": 161.7,
          "end": 164.67000000000002,
          "en": "So you assign a name to a variable",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 52,
          "start": 164.67000000000002,
          "end": 168.12,
          "en": "and you can then use this variable name as an identifier",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 53,
          "start": 168.12,
          "end": 171.48,
          "en": "in your code to refer to that value that's stored",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 54,
          "start": 171.48,
          "end": 176.34,
          "en": "in this variable whenever you need to use that value.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 55,
          "start": 176.34,
          "end": 178.56,
          "en": "And you typically use variables",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 56,
          "start": 178.56,
          "end": 182.04,
          "en": "because they allow you to reuse a value",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 57,
          "start": 182.04,
          "end": 185.52,
          "en": "and because they can help with code readability",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 58,
          "start": 185.52,
          "end": 189.42,
          "en": "because often it makes sense to not define the value",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 59,
          "start": 189.42,
          "end": 192.48,
          "en": "in the place where it's needed, but maybe instead ahead",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 60,
          "start": 192.48,
          "end": 196.05,
          "en": "of time so that your overall code stays a bit cleaner",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 61,
          "start": 196.05,
          "end": 197.46,
          "en": "and more readable.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 62,
          "start": 197.46,
          "end": 200.49,
          "en": "Now, in JavaScript variables can be created",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 63,
          "start": 200.49,
          "end": 202.62,
          "en": "with the let keyword here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 64,
          "start": 202.62,
          "end": 205.53,
          "en": "I could create a variable named user message",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 65,
          "start": 205.53,
          "end": 207.87,
          "en": "though the name is up to you",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 66,
          "start": 207.87,
          "end": 210.753,
          "en": "it just should follow certain rules.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 67,
          "start": 212.73,
          "end": 215.61,
          "en": "For example, it should be written using this camel",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 68,
          "start": 215.61,
          "end": 219.84,
          "en": "case notation where you start with a lowercase character.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 69,
          "start": 219.84,
          "end": 223.98,
          "en": "And if your variable name then consists of multiple words",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 70,
          "start": 223.98,
          "end": 225.93,
          "en": "every word inside of the word",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 71,
          "start": 225.93,
          "end": 229.45,
          "en": "like message here starts with a capital case character",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 72,
          "start": 230.4,
          "end": 234.09,
          "en": "and it must not contain any dashes or white space.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 73,
          "start": 234.09,
          "end": 236.67000000000002,
          "en": "So this variable name would be invalid just",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 74,
          "start": 236.67000000000002,
          "end": 240.27,
          "en": "as this name would be, underscores would be allowed",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 75,
          "start": 240.27,
          "end": 242.52,
          "en": "but are not common in JavaScript.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 76,
          "start": 242.52,
          "end": 243.54,
          "en": "Instead, as mentioned",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 77,
          "start": 243.54,
          "end": 245.613,
          "en": "the convention is to use this notation.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 78,
          "start": 246.54,
          "end": 249.87,
          "en": "Variable names also may contain numbers",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 79,
          "start": 249.87,
          "end": 252,
          "en": "but not at the beginning.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 80,
          "start": 252,
          "end": 252.833,
          "en": "In addition",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 81,
          "start": 252.833,
          "end": 255.24,
          "en": "they also may not contain any special characters",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 82,
          "start": 255.24,
          "end": 258.24,
          "en": "like an exclamation mark or a question mark.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 83,
          "start": 258.24,
          "end": 262.14,
          "en": "The only exception is the dollar sign and the underscore",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 84,
          "start": 262.14,
          "end": 263.44,
          "en": "which I already mentioned.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 85,
          "start": 264.3,
          "end": 266.79,
          "en": "But other than that, the name is up to you.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 86,
          "start": 266.79,
          "end": 269.37,
          "en": "And then I could store this hello world text",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 87,
          "start": 269.37,
          "end": 271.29,
          "en": "in user message.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 88,
          "start": 271.29,
          "end": 274.23,
          "en": "If I then want to use this value, for example here",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 89,
          "start": 274.23,
          "end": 277.08,
          "en": "for logging it to the console, I would simply use the name",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 90,
          "start": 277.08,
          "end": 281.43,
          "en": "of the variable and thereby refer to this value.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 91,
          "start": 281.43,
          "end": 283.29,
          "en": "And that's how I use a variable.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 92,
          "start": 283.29,
          "end": 286.83,
          "en": "And if I now want to lock this multiple times, for example",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 93,
          "start": 286.83,
          "end": 291.03,
          "en": "I gain this reusability advantage by using a variable",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 94,
          "start": 291.03,
          "end": 295.38,
          "en": "because instead of having to copy and paste that value",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 95,
          "start": 295.38,
          "end": 299.37,
          "en": "I just define it once and then use that variable as often",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 96,
          "start": 299.37,
          "end": 303.9,
          "en": "as I want to use that value that has the advantage that",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 97,
          "start": 303.9,
          "end": 307.38,
          "en": "if that value ever needs to change, I only need to change it",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 98,
          "start": 307.38,
          "end": 309.99,
          "en": "in one place instead of multiple places.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 99,
          "start": 309.99,
          "end": 313.558,
          "en": "And all the places in my code where I use that variable",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 100,
          "start": 313.558,
          "end": 318.36,
          "en": "will automatically use that changed value, with that.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 101,
          "start": 318.36,
          "end": 321.84,
          "en": "If I reload this, I of course see hello world being output",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 102,
          "start": 321.84,
          "end": 324.003,
          "en": "in the console twice.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 103,
          "start": 326.31,
          "end": 328.623,
          "en": "So that's how we create variables.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 104,
          "start": 329.76,
          "end": 333.06,
          "en": "Now, related to variables, we also have constants",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 105,
          "start": 333.06,
          "end": 335.553,
          "en": "which are created with the const keyword.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 106,
          "start": 336.45,
          "end": 341.45,
          "en": "If I save this and reload, I again see HelloWorld being",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 107,
          "start": 342.57,
          "end": 345.15,
          "en": "output twice here.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 108,
          "start": 345.15,
          "end": 349.59000000000003,
          "en": "So constants work like variables, they are data containers.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 109,
          "start": 349.59000000000003,
          "end": 353.7,
          "en": "But the key differences that constants must not be",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 110,
          "start": 353.7,
          "end": 358.7,
          "en": "reassigned, if I try to assign a new value,",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 111,
          "start": 358.77,
          "end": 361.14,
          "en": "a new string, for example, like this",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 112,
          "start": 361.14,
          "end": 362.52,
          "en": "I'm getting an error",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 113,
          "start": 362.52,
          "end": 366.183,
          "en": "that it's read only because it's a constant.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 114,
          "start": 367.59,
          "end": 371.22,
          "en": "If I were using let here instead of const",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 115,
          "start": 371.22,
          "end": 372.93,
          "en": "everything would work instead.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 116,
          "start": 372.93,
          "end": 375.813,
          "en": "Now I would again see my log here in the console.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 117,
          "start": 376.92,
          "end": 378.45,
          "en": "So that's the key difference",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 118,
          "start": 378.45,
          "end": 382.89,
          "en": "with let, I can use this single equal sign operator to",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 119,
          "start": 382.89,
          "end": 386.043,
          "en": "assign a new value, with const, I can't.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 120,
          "start": 387.36,
          "end": 390.06,
          "en": "Now, there are different philosophies out there.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 121,
          "start": 390.06,
          "end": 391.56,
          "en": "In the JavaScript world",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 122,
          "start": 391.56,
          "end": 395.55,
          "en": "certain developers like to use const as often as possible",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 123,
          "start": 395.55,
          "end": 398.01,
          "en": "and I am one of those developers.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 124,
          "start": 398.01,
          "end": 400.86,
          "en": "Other developers want to use let all the time",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 125,
          "start": 400.86,
          "end": 402.9,
          "en": "because it has less characters",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 126,
          "start": 402.9,
          "end": 405.693,
          "en": "and therefore requires less code to type.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 127,
          "start": 406.77,
          "end": 409.98,
          "en": "But I prefer to be clear about my intentions",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 128,
          "start": 409.98,
          "end": 413.31,
          "en": "and if I have a value that never should be reassigned.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 129,
          "start": 413.31,
          "end": 416.19,
          "en": "I personally prefer to use const.",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 130,
          "start": 416.19,
          "end": 417.69,
          "en": "You can use whatever you want",
          "zh": ""
        },
        {
          "id": 131,
          "start": 417.69,
          "end": 419.763,
          "en": "but you should know about the difference.",
          "zh": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "errors": []
}